window.docContents[70].resolve({"/Foundations/Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Tempered-Distributions--Convolution-and-translation":{"contents":"For complex-valued Schwartz functions , their\nconvolutionis again a Schwartz function. For fixed , the map  is\ncontinuous and complex linear.\n\nThe convolution theorem is what identifies the Fourier-side definition of\n with the integral convolution when  is itself a Schwartz\nfunction.\n\nThe Fourier transform converts convolution of Schwartz functions into\npointwise multiplication:\n\nMathlib defines Schwartz convolution through inverse Fourier transform of the\npointwise product. Fourier inversion therefore reduces the displayed identity\nto the corresponding pairing identity in Schwartz space.\n\nDefining  on the Fourier side avoids any integral representation\nof  and makes the Fourier characterization hold by construction.\n\nFor a scalar Schwartz function  and a tempered distribution ,\ntheir convolution is the tempered distribution defined byFor fixed , the map  is continuous and complex\nlinear.\n\nConvolution with a Schwartz function has the expected Fourier characterization:\n\nApply Fourier transform to the defining inverse Fourier transform and use the\nFourier inversion law on tempered distributions. The remaining factor is\nMathlib's continuous multiplier action by the Schwartz function\n.\n\nA Fourier-side definition needs a compatibility check: it must agree with the\nintegral convolution when both objects are functions. Injectivity of the Fourier\ntransform reduces the check to the Schwartz convolution theorem.\n\nIf  and  are Schwartz functions and  is the regular\ntempered distribution induced by , then\n\nFourier transform is injective on tempered distributions. After transforming\nboth sides, the left side is the regular distribution \nmultiplied by the Schwartz function . Its pairing integral\nis that of the density , which Mathlib's\nSchwartz convolution theorem also gives on the right. Associativity and\ncommutativity of complex multiplication identify the two pairing integrals.\n\nFor  with  a complex normed space,\ndistributional differentiation commutes with convolution by a scalar\nSchwartz function:\n\nApply the injective Fourier transform. Both sides become multiplication of\n by the Fourier derivative symbol and by .\nThe two scalar multipliers commute pointwise.\n\nTranslation is the second transport operation, and its sign convention is\nfixed by the pairing.\n\nFor , translation of a tempered distribution is the continuous\ncomplex-linear equivalence determined byFor a regular distribution induced by , this convention corresponds to\n.\n\nTempered translations form an additive action and translation by  is the\ninverse of translation by :\n\nOn Schwartz test functions, two successive precompositions by subtraction of\n and  equal precomposition by subtraction of . Transposing this\nidentity gives the composition law. The zero and inverse laws are its\nspecializations.\n\nConstant-coefficient operators commute with translations, and the directional\nderivative is the basic instance.\n\nDistributional directional derivatives commute with translation:\n\nMove the derivative onto a Schwartz test function. Mathlib's Fréchet chain rule\nfor the translated test function identifies its directional derivative with\nthe translation of the original directional derivative. Transposing this\nidentity gives the displayed formula.\n\nThe support law makes the sign convention visible geometrically. Vanishing sets\ncorrespond exactly under , so the law is an equality rather than\nan inclusion.\n\nUnder the convention , translation moves support by\n:\n\nA translated distribution vanishes on an open set precisely when the original\ndistribution vanishes on its preimage under . Applying this\nequivalence to neighborhoods and then taking complements gives the exact\nsupport image.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Distributions and Fourier Analysis\u0009Tempered Distributions","header":"3.4.2.2. Convolution and translation","id":"/Foundations/Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Tempered-Distributions--Convolution-and-translation"},"/Related-Work/#PDEAtlas--Related-Work--FloatLib___-exact-algebra___-and-CEGA":{"contents":"FloatLib is our library for connecting executable floating-point arithmetic to\nexact specifications and theorems about decoded real values\n. Its\nofficial guide\ndevelops this connection through arithmetic and interval examples. TorchLean\nalso uses FloatLib , applying the arithmetic\nlibrary in a different mathematical development.\n\nIn PDEAtlas, exact symbolic computation supplies identities, derivatives, and\nanalytic side conditions. CEGA's ordinary interval queries use rational\nendpoints and exact rational calculations to certify bounds on real functions.\nFloatLib supplies their interval arithmetic, elementary-function enclosures,\nand outward rounding. PDEAtlas's rational interval adds an endpoint-order proof\nto FloatLib.Numerics.Interval ℚ. For generic expression evaluation, a\nsemantic translation maps CAS syntax into FloatLib's own expression language;\nupstream backends supply the endpoint representation and numerical operations.\nTheir containment theorems cover arbitrary real members of the input intervals.\nFailed decoding, unsupported domains, or unrepresentable output bounds return\nno enclosure.\n\nFloatLib also provides certified IEEE binary32 operations; CEGA's concrete\nbackend is rational and does not dispatch to them.\n\nThe connection to the CAS is explicit in the derivative-enclosure bridge.\nPDEAtlas's CompiledDerivative certificate supplies the derivative expression\nand its analytic justification on the chosen panel. The numerical evaluator\nmust enclose that same expression, with an input interval covering the whole\npanel. Its soundness theorem then gives a bound on the requested derivative.\nMore generally, the shared real-interval interface allows a proved enclosure\nto produce CEGA enclosure and residual certificates. The bridge requires a\nproof that the original real function lies in the interval throughout the\nstated domain.\n\nFloatLib proves an exponential enclosure using argument reduction and a Taylor remainder,\nthen rounds its bounds outward when the chosen representation requires it.\nPDEAtlas connects that containment theorem to the original symbolic expression\nand its domain. Derivative and quadrature certificates add the analytic\nregularity and remainder estimates needed for the surrounding PDE argument.\n\nThe numerical APIs also contain scalar and piecewise Taylor approximation\ncertificates, whose general constructors take real Taylor coefficients and so\nare noncomputable; the derivative enclosures and rational error calculations\nthat feed them are executable. The polynomial subsystem includes a global root\nisolator built from checked square-free reduction, a generated Cauchy bound,\nand Sturm subdivision. When its finite search succeeds, the resulting atlas\ncovers every distinct real root.\n\nArmstrong and collaborators describe extensive model assistance under human\nsupervision ; Tau Ceti organizes assisted\nformalization around a shared library .\nPDEAtlas aims to make its Sobolev, weak-solution, and certified-estimate\ninfrastructure reusable by other Mathlib-based PDE projects, and to prepare\nsuitable components for review in Mathlib and Tau Ceti.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Related Work","header":"2.6. FloatLib, exact algebra, and CEGA","id":"/Related-Work/#PDEAtlas--Related-Work--FloatLib___-exact-algebra___-and-CEGA"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Representing-Solutions/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points","header":"9.2. Representing Solutions","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Representing-Solutions/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions"}});